The easiest way to accomplish this is probably to teach the cmene-form as a 
name-introductory form along with the commonly used "mi'e". Like in lesson 
zero in the wavelessonscontinued! Or start using a cmene introductory form 
yourself!

I would call this a rather small "problem" since all it takes is one 
explanation to get it to stick. I mean, it's not that complicated to 
understand. It's analogous to "di'u" and "la'e di'u", so it could be taught 
along side that as well.

